dsprenkels/sss — Shamir secret sharing (hazmat / low-level GF(256) share math)

Upstream:        https://github.com/dsprenkels/sss
Pinned commit:   9f2a10b1c0d391bed485c4c7e93788ce37c360f1
License:         MIT (see ./LICENSE)

Vendored files:  hazmat.c, hazmat.h   (ONLY the constant-time GF(256) share math)
NOT vendored:    sss.c, tweetnacl.c, randombytes.c  (virgil-crypto-c builds the
                 authenticated envelope on the in-tree mbedTLS instead, and
                 supplies entropy via its own RNG interface).

Local modifications (kept minimal for auditability against upstream):
  1. Public functions prefixed `vscf_sss_*`
     (sss_create_keyshares -> vscf_sss_create_keyshares,
      sss_combine_keyshares -> vscf_sss_combine_keyshares)
     to avoid symbol collisions in the flat multi-wrapper symbol table.
  2. `#include "randombytes.h"` removed. `vscf_sss_create_keyshares` now takes
     the random polynomial coefficients as a `const uint8_t *random_data`
     parameter (32 * (k - 1) bytes), supplied by the caller (vscf_shamir) from
     virgil's RNG. This removes the randombytes dependency and keeps the
     function thread-safe and deterministic given its inputs.

The GF(256) arithmetic itself is byte-identical to upstream and is constant
time. Do not "optimize" it.

To re-vendor / bump: fetch hazmat.{c,h} at the new commit, re-apply the two
modifications above, and update the pinned commit hash here.
